<h3>Summary in Three Lines</h3>
<ul>
    <li>CLAUDE.md is <strong>a set of behavioral guidelines Claude Code reads automatically every session</strong> &mdash; the ten commandments introduced here are general-purpose guidelines that <strong>take direct aim at the ten mistakes LLMs make most often</strong> when they write code.</li>
    <li>The structure has three stages &mdash; <strong>a careful start</strong> (think first; plan/checklist/context notes), <strong>code-writing principles</strong> (simplicity, surgical changes, header comments, punctuation, tests), and <strong>verification and wrap-up</strong> (goal-driven, semantic commits, reading errors closely).</li>
    <li>Using it is simple &mdash; <strong>save the original below at your project root as <code>CLAUDE.md</code></strong>, then add your project's own instructions. You confirm the effect by watching your diffs get cleaner.</li>
</ul>

<h3>How to Use It &mdash; Three Minutes to Install, a Lifetime of Nagging</h3>
<p>Here is the analogy. A new hire who is highly skilled but whose <strong>working habits are still a blank page</strong> shows up fresh every morning (an AI's memory resets each session). Instead of teaching them out loud every time, you <strong>post ten house rules on their desk</strong> &mdash; that is CLAUDE.md.</p>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Step 1 &middot; Paste it in</strong> &mdash; copy the entire English original below and save it at your repository root under the name <code>CLAUDE.md</code>. That's all. Claude Code will read it on its own from the next conversation onward. (To use it as a personal global rule, put it in <code>~/.claude/CLAUDE.md</code> and it applies to every project &mdash; for the scope story, see <a href="#!ccb-claudemd">CLAUDE.md &mdash; The Instruction File</a>.)</li>
    <li><strong>Step 2 &middot; Add your project's flavor</strong> &mdash; the original says right in its first line to merge it with project-specific instructions. Append your team's rules below: build commands, coding conventions, things that are off-limits. If the ten commandments are the &lsquo;constitution&rsquo;, project instructions are the &lsquo;enforcement decree&rsquo;.</li>
    <li><strong>Step 3 &middot; Spread it to the team</strong> &mdash; commit the file to the repository and every teammate's AI becomes <strong>a colleague working by the same rules</strong>. You'll hear less grumbling in review along the lines of &ldquo;the AI touched the neighboring code again&rdquo;.</li>
    <li><strong>How to tell it's listening</strong> &mdash; exactly by the criteria at the end of the original. If (1) <strong>unnecessary changes in diffs go down</strong>, (2) rewrites caused by overcomplication go down, and (3) <strong>questions arrive before mistakes</strong>, the guidelines are doing their job.</li>
    <li><strong>One spoonful of caution</strong> &mdash; as the original admits itself, these guidelines <strong>lean toward caution over speed</strong>. Demanding the three artifacts even for trivial work like a one-off script can be stifling, so in those cases trust the &ldquo;judgment&rdquo; clause and travel light.</li>
</ul>

